From: TANYA@alcor.org
Date:  Mon, 26 Sep 94 14:11:10 
Subject: [#94-9-526] Cryonics and Metabolic Dependent State Loss

Something which might be of interest in this discussion:

Isolated cat brains were treated with 15% glycerol (resulting in the 
freezing of approximately 60% v/v of brain fluids) and cooled to -20*C 
for five days, then rewarmed and reperfused with blood.  After this 
cryoprotectant treatment, freezing, and rewarming, the brains 
demonstrated the return of normal brain activity (as determined by 
EEG measurements).
see:  Suda I, Kito A.C.: Viability of long-term frozen cat brain in vitro, 
*Nature*, 212, 270 (1966)

I think that Suda also recorded electrical activity in brains that had 
been frozen for 273 days, either in the paper cited above or another.  
(I'll look into it, if anyone is interested.)

Also relevent is Audrey Smith's work in the 50's, including the 
experiment in which golden hamsters were cooled until 60% of their 
brain water was converted to ice.  Subsequent rewarming resulted in 
recovery with no apparent behavioral abnormalities.  (Of course, no 
one could ask them how they felt. . . :-)
see:  Smith A.U.: Studies on golden hamsters during cooling to and 
rewarming from body temperatures below 0*C, *Proc. Royal Soc., 
Biology, (London) Series B*, 147, 517 (1957)

There are many more interesting references to the efficacy of 
freezing,  many of them more current than the two listed here.  I'd be 
happy to provide more information if anyone is interested, either in 
private or on the List.

BTW, cryogenics is more specifically, the science of low temperature 
engineering.  I think this includes things like superconductors.

--Tanya

